WEBVTT

00:03.960 --> 00:07.960
Now we have different types of subqueries based on the results.

00:07.960 --> 00:11.640
So this means the amount of data that the subquery can return.

00:11.640 --> 00:14.680
So the first type is the scalar subquery.

00:14.680 --> 00:20.920
So it is a subquery that is going to return only one single value like for example the value three.

00:20.960 --> 00:23.640
Let's have an example for the scalar subquery.

00:23.840 --> 00:29.080
So in this query for example if you are saying select star you will get all columns all the rows from

00:29.080 --> 00:29.760
one table.

00:29.760 --> 00:33.120
But for the scalar subquery we need only one value.

00:33.120 --> 00:36.280
So how we usually get it is by doing some aggregations.

00:36.280 --> 00:40.440
For example, if you go and say let's get the average of sales.

00:40.440 --> 00:41.920
So let's execute it.

00:41.920 --> 00:45.600
And with that in the output we have only one value with 38.

00:45.640 --> 00:48.760
We call such a query as a scalar query.

00:48.760 --> 00:51.720
So it has only one row and only one column.

00:51.720 --> 00:53.600
So this is a scalar query.

00:53.640 --> 00:54.040
All right.

00:54.040 --> 00:57.720
So now to the second type we have the row subquery.

00:57.720 --> 01:02.960
So it is a subquery that is going to return multiple rows and a single column.

01:02.960 --> 01:05.720
So we're going to have like values 123.

01:05.720 --> 01:08.280
So it is only one column with multiple rows.

01:08.320 --> 01:10.280
Let's have an example for the row query.

01:10.320 --> 01:13.880
As you can see now we are seeing select star from the table orders.

01:13.880 --> 01:17.700
And now we are getting multiple rows and multiple columns.

01:17.700 --> 01:19.340
But for the row queries we need.

01:19.380 --> 01:20.500
Only one column.

01:20.500 --> 01:21.380
So you can go over here.

01:21.380 --> 01:26.060
For example say customer ID and if you go and execute it.

01:26.060 --> 01:30.620
So now if you check the output we have a single column and as well multiple rows.

01:30.620 --> 01:32.940
So we have like a list of values.

01:32.940 --> 01:35.460
And this is what we call a row query.

01:35.500 --> 01:35.860
All right.

01:35.860 --> 01:38.900
So now to the last type we have the table subquery.

01:38.940 --> 01:45.260
It's going to go and return multiple rows and as well multiple columns like any regular tables.

01:45.260 --> 01:48.420
So this subquery can return a lot of values okay.

01:48.460 --> 01:51.260
So let's see an example of that table query.

01:51.260 --> 01:54.340
So if you check our example here select star from orders.

01:54.340 --> 01:58.980
We got here multiple rows and as well multiple columns.

01:59.020 --> 02:05.540
And of course we can go and select multiple columns like for example the order ID and the order date.

02:06.340 --> 02:10.140
So if we execute it here in the output we have multiple columns.

02:10.140 --> 02:12.540
We have two columns and as well multiple rows.

02:12.580 --> 02:16.020
That's why this kind of query is as well a table query.

02:16.020 --> 02:16.380
All right.

02:16.380 --> 02:20.940
So with that we have learned that different types of subqueries based on the result type.

02:20.940 --> 02:26.580
Now we're going to go and learn how to use the subqueries in different locations in our query.

02:26.580 --> 02:30.700
So we're going to start with how to use subquery in the From clause.
